course: dictionary
lesson: 5444
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Lance is a good example: it can be a long spear, a word for cutting open a boil, or a boy's name. Context tells you which meaning is right. «The knight lowered his lance and charged across the field.» The knight, the charge and the field tell you it is the weapon.
explain: Look at the words around it: «The doctor had to lance the boil with a clean, sharp tool.» Doctor, boil and clean tool show that lance is a verb meaning to cut it open so the pus can come out. Now test it: does «The doctor had to spear the boil» make sense? No, so this is a different meaning.
explain: Capital letters in the middle of a sentence are another clue. «Yesterday I played football with Lance.» Lance is a name, because it is a person. Each time you meet a word with more than one meaning, ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
fact: A lance is a long spear with a pointed tip.
fact: Knights carried lances on horseback in battles and jousts.
fact: As a verb, «lance» means to cut open a boil so the pus can come out. A doctor does this with clean, sharp tools.
fact: Never try to lance a boil yourself. Ask an adult to get a doctor.
fact: Lance is also a boy's name, and a soldier's rank: a lance corporal.
exercise: «At tournaments knights jousted, charging at each other on horseback with blunted lances.» What is a lance here? | A kind of horse | A sharp tool used by a doctor | A boy's name | *A long spear carried by a knight
exercise: «The doctor had to lance the boil with a clean, sharp tool.» What does lance mean here? | *To cut open so the pus can come out | To name a new baby | To hold a spear ready for battle | To ride a horse very fast
exercise: «Yesterday I played football with Lance.» In this sentence, what is Lance? | To cut something open | A long spear | *A boy's name | A sharp tool
test: «The knights couched their lances and waited for the signal.» What does couch a lance mean? | *To hold it ready | To polish it until it shines | To break it in two | To throw it far away
test: Why should you never try to lance a boil yourself? | *Because it needs a doctor with clean, sharp tools | Because it is a fun game | Because lancing means naming a baby | Because boils are good for you
exam: Which meaning fits best in «The knight raised his lance and the crowd cheered»? | *A long spear carried on horseback | A boy's name | To cut open a boil | To rest on a hill
